{"name":"Oceanic Motion","hex":"#1d5c83","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"oceanic","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of or relating to the ocean.","Living in, produced by, or frequenting the ocean."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/oceanic"},{"term":"motion","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A change whereby something goes from one place to another; a state of progression from one place to another; a change of position with respect to time.","A parliamentary action to propose something.  A similar procedure in any official or business meeting."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To gesture indicating a desired movement.","To introduce a motion in parliamentary procedure."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/motion"}],"entries":[{"title":"Motion","blocks":[{"text":"In physics, motion is the change in position of an object or fluid with respect to a reference frame over a given time. Motion is mathematically described in terms of vector quantities such as displacement, velocity, acceleration, etc. The relative motion of an object with respect to an observer is the object's motion described in the observer's comoving frame, quantified in terms of relative position, relative velocity, etc. The branch of physics describing the motion of objects without reference to their cause is called kinematics, while the branch studying forces and their effect on motion is called dynamics."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/Porsche_race_car_Verschuur_amk.jpg/330px-Porsche_race_car_Verschuur_amk.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Motion","wikidata":"Q79782"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/45/MontreGousset001.jpg/500px-MontreGousset001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikiped